      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We start by multiplying both sides of the equation by $(x+2)$:\n\\begin{align*}\n5(x+2) &= x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x \\\\\n5x + 10 &= x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x \\\\\n0 &= x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x - 5x - 10 \\\\\n0 &= x^3 - 2x^2 - 13x - 10\n\\end{align*}\nWe can factor out an $x$ from each term: $x(x^2 - 2x - 13) - 10$.\nWe cannot find any real factors of $x^2 - 2x - 13$ by factoring, so we try to complete the square:\n\\begin{align*}\nx^2 - 2x - 13 &= (x - 1)^2 - 14 \\\\\n(x - 1)^2 &= 14\n\\end{align*}\nSolving for $x$, we find that $x = 1 + \\sqrt{14}$ and $x = 1 - \\sqrt{14}$.\nThe sum of these $x$-values is $(1 + \\sqrt{14}) + (1 - \\sqrt{14}) = \\boxed{2}$.The answer is: 2",
