      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "First, we simplify the given equation by multiplying both sides by $x+2$:\n$5(x+2)=x^3-2x^2-8x$\n$5x+10=x^3-2x^2-8x$\nNext, we subtract $x^3-2x^2-8x$ from both sides:\n$5x+10-(x^3-2x^2-8x)=0$\n$-x^3+2x^2-3x+10=0$\nTo solve for the $x$-values, we can factor the cubic equation or use the rational root theorem to find any rational roots. In this case, we find that $x=1$ is a root, so we can use synthetic division to divide the cubic polynomial by $(x-1)$:\n$\\begin{array}{c|ccccc}\n1 & -1 & 2 & -3 & 10 \\\\\n\\hline\n& & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\\n\\hline\n& -1 & 3 & 0 & 0\n\\end{array}$\nThe factorization of $-x^3+2x^2-3x+10$ is $(x-1)(-x^2+3x+10)$.\nThen, we set each factor equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n$x-1=0$ gives $x=1$\n$-x^2+3x+10=0$\n$(-x+5)(x+2)=0$\n$x=5$ or $x=-2$\nThe three roots of the given equation are $x=1$, $x=5$, $x=-2$, and $x=4$. The sum of these roots is $1+5+(-2)+4=\\boxed{8}$.\nThe answer is: 8",
